    "question": "CJ, KJ, and AJ collect stamps.  CJ has 5 more than twice the number of stamps that KJ has, and KJ has half as many as AJ.  If the three boys have 930 stamps all together, how many stamps does AJ have?",
    "gold": "370",
    "answer": "Let x represent the number of stamps for AJ\nKJ:x/2 stamps\nCJ:5+2(x/2)=5+x\nTotal:x+(x/2)+5+x=930\n(5/2)x+5=930\n(5/2)x=925\nx=925(2/5)=370 stamps\n#### 370"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "Let's denote the number of stamps that KJ has as \\( K \\). According to the problem, KJ has half as many stamps as AJ, so AJ has \\( 2K \\) stamps. Additionally, CJ has 5 more than twice the number of stamps that KJ has, so CJ has \\( 2K + 5 \\) stamps.\n\nThe total number of stamps that CJ, KJ, and AJ have together is 930. Therefore, we can write the following equation:\n\n\\[\nK + 2K + (2K + 5) = 930\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nK + 2K + 2K + 5 = 930\n\\]\n\\[\n5K + 5 = 930\n\\]\n\nNext, we isolate \\( K \\) by subtracting 5 from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n5K = 925\n\\]\n\nThen, we solve for \\( K \\) by dividing both sides by 5:\n\n\\[\nK = 185\n\\]\n\nNow that we know KJ has 185 stamps, we can find out how many stamps AJ has. Since AJ has twice as many stamps as KJ, we calculate:\n\n\\[\n2K = 2 \\times 185 = 370\n\\]\n\nTherefore, AJ has \\(\\boxed{370}\\) stamps.",
